entry roentgen
term: Roentgen (R)
difficulty: advanced
related_terms: sievert
related_terms: gray
related_terms: exposure
related_terms: x-ray
related_terms: geiger-counter
quick_facts: Measures ionization in air, not dose to people
quick_facts: 1 R = 2.58×10⁻⁴ C/kg of air
quick_facts: Honors Wilhelm Röntgen, first Physics Nobel (1901)
quick_facts: Roughly comparable to a rem for early gamma exposures
quick_facts: Still seen on vintage meters and in Chernobyl accounts

sources:
    {"organization":"BIPM","title":"SI Units","url":"https://www.bipm.org/en/measurement-units"}

definition:
    A legacy unit measuring radiation exposure — strictly, the ionization produced in dry air by X-rays or gamma rays: 1 R equals 2.58×10⁻⁴ coulombs of charge per kilogram of air. Named after Wilhelm Röntgen, discoverer of X-rays and winner of the first Nobel Prize in Physics (1901). It measures what radiation does to air, not to tissue, and has been superseded — though it lingers in old instruments, film badges, and disaster folklore.

debunking_snippet:
    “Roentgens and sieverts are interchangeable.” They measure different things: R counts ionization in air; sieverts weigh biological effect on tissue. Old meters read R — converting needs care and context.
